notes['T091'].update(finding=notes['T091']['finding']+' A P/2=24.267-d harmonic predicts another ~54-ppt deep event in sector 26 at BTJD 2025.2678. The raw core has 12 uninterrupted cadences (93% expected coverage) and only a 1.0-ppt deficit; the right side is fully covered and the left side 62% covered. The plotted window excludes a comparable deep eclipse there, though it is not a strict three-band shallow-event null.')
notes['T093'].update(verdict='PLAUSIBLE',finding='Two deep flat-bottomed raw eclipses recur at 2312.9773 and 3044.2421, 731.265 d apart. A sector-10 partial low state may be a third occasion but cannot supply a center; a sector-38 shallower eclipse is of the opposite kind. Thirteen cycles would give 56.25114 d, near Gaia 56.2262 d, but neighboring 12- and 14-cycle counts have no sensitive predicted primary windows in the available data. The opposite-kind phase constrains the clock only if the Gaia orbit and its eccentricity are assumed. Shorter 39/65-cycle harmonics have visually flat covered predicted deep windows.',limits='The integer cycle count and an eclipse-derived orbital period remain unresolved. The sector-10 partial state cannot time a cycle. QLP KSPSAP suppresses the long event; the independent TESScut aperture recovers it.',next='Measure another full deep eclipse or obtain an independent period constraint. The 56.25114-d value remains conditional only.')
notes['T095'].update(verdict='PLAUSIBLE',finding='Two deep raw eclipses at 1921.7669 and 2611.8693 recur after 690.102 d. A shallower eclipse at 1907.3921 is a distinct kind. Twenty-four deep cycles would give 28.7543 d near Gaia 28.7717 d; a 72-cycle short harmonic predicts a visually absent deep event at 2631.0388, but neighboring 23/25-cycle aliases have no sensitive predicted primary windows. The shallow event cannot force a half phase without assuming the Gaia orbit.',limits='The two deep occasions do not uniquely fix the cycle count. The shallow eclipse is 3.47 Gaia linear-sigma from nominal opposite conjunction, so Gaia-orbit association remains insecure. KSPSAP suppresses long dips; TESScut recovers one.',next='Measure another deep eclipse to establish the clock and test Gaia association.')
notes['T097'].update(verdict='PLAUSIBLE',finding='Two ~225-ppt raw primary-like eclipses occur at 1559.4177 and 2275.4556, 716.038 d apart. A partly covered shallower ~56-ppt event near 3737.73 is a third, opposite-kind candidate, but its center is insecure. Twelve cycles would give 59.6698 d near Gaia 59.6703 d; 36/48/60-cycle short harmonics have actual flat predicted primary windows, while neighboring 11/13-cycle aliases remain without sensitive nulls. One opposite-kind event cannot independently select the 12-cycle count.',limits='The sector-89 shallow event is partly gapped and cannot supply a precision midpoint. Sector 62 is an unusable negative-target-pixel cutout, not a null. Orbital period and Gaia association are not established.',next='Time an additional fully covered primary to distinguish neighboring cycle counts.')
notes['T098'].update(period='34.786',error='0.002',finding=notes['T098']['finding']+' The P/2=17.3929-d alias predicts a deep eclipse at BTJD 1812.8054 in fully covered sector-18 raw SAP, where the light curve is flat through the expected 1.14-d core. Deep and shallow pairs independently give 34.78577 and 34.78698 d; adopt P=34.786 d with a conservative 0.002-d allowance. The selected clock also matches the eccentric Gaia conjunction spacing.',limits='The 0.002-d allowance covers the difference between two kind-specific pair periods; it is conservative, not calibrated one-sigma precision. KSPSAP suppresses broad dips; TESScut custom aperture confirms a representative event.')
notes['T099'].update(period='28.791',error='0.001',epoch='1822.7651',finding='A deep class appears at 1822.7651, 2916.8088 and 3291.0574; a shallower class at 1837.1432 and 2931.2046. The first two deep events give 28.79062 d over 38 cycles and the shallow pair gives 28.79109 d over 38 cycles. The sector-73 deep profile is observed through ingress, core and egress; its nearest 0.220-d gap falls after egress. Its fitted center differs from the two-kind prediction by 0.041 d, within a conservative 0.05-d center allowance. Adopt P=28.791 d with a conservative 0.001-d allowance, rather than the overprecise all-three formal fit. The P/2 alias maps deep and shallow events to the same kind despite their distinct persistent depths.',limits='The 0.001-d period allowance is conservative, not a calibrated one-sigma error. Broad baseline gaps and slopes affect the sector-73 center; lead-t099-bounds.json records actual timing coverage and the 0.05-d bound.',next='Finish unavailable novelty catalogue gates before any new-system claim.')
notes['T100'].update(period='',error='',epoch='',finding='Many narrow dips recur on the target-favored pixels across sectors 7, 34, 61 and 88. The raw SAP folded at 0.67918 d shows two near-equal dip trains separated by half a cycle, including sector-7 centers only 0.3336 d apart. Folding at 0.33959 d combines those trains. The supported photometric dip interval is about 0.3396 d; the orbital period could be that value or twice it (about 0.6792 d) because the two kinds are not securely distinguishable. Either clock is far from the selecting Gaia 58.8274-d SB1 orbit, so this is a separate short-period eclipsing signal or contaminant, not evidence that the Gaia spectroscopic orbit eclipses.',limits='Photometric recurrence is secure, but the orbital harmonic and physical relationship to the Gaia SB1 remain unresolved. The long-cycle formal timing error is inappropriate; no orbital period or error is adopted.',next='Resolve the orbital harmonic and source relation with spectroscopy or higher-resolution light curves.')
